+# Riverton Three-Year Heat Mitigation Brief
+
+## Decision
+
+- Audience: Riverton City Council Climate Committee
+- Lead intervention: Option A — a city-supported cool-roof program
+- Complementary measure: Trees and vegetation
+- Geography: East Ward only
+- Horizon: Three years
+
+The committee should adopt Option A for existing multifamily housing because the packet identifies a concentrated late-night indoor-heat problem, a group of plausible roof candidates, and a program model capable of producing measurable building-level outputs within three years. [Housing Heat Scan] [Options Memo] Tree planting should proceed alongside the roof work for shade and longer-term benefits, but the packet does not project a three-year indoor-temperature effect from trees. [Options Memo] This recommendation is a reason to test and measure the approach, not a claim that it has already succeeded.
+
+## Evidence comparison
+
+| Intervention | Observed support | Projection | Limit |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Cool roofs | In East Ward, 26 of 40 monitored apartments crossed the late-night threshold, the median maximum overnight temperature was 84.8°F, and seven participating buildings had dark low-slope roofs that appeared to merit further screening. [Housing Heat Scan] | Option A models seven completed roofs totaling 210,000 square feet and a decline to 82.8°F or lower for the monitored treated-building group. [Options Memo] | The scan did not test coating compatibility or show that roof characteristics caused indoor temperatures; the modeled change is not observed. |
+| Trees and vegetation | Forty-nine percent of survey respondents selected more trees or courtyard shade as a preferred action, with multiple answers allowed. [Resident Survey] | Option A proposes 120 trees and a Year 3 survival target, but projects no three-year indoor-temperature effect from them. [Options Memo] | Survival, growth, species, siting, weather, and maintenance affect future shade, so planting counts are not evidence of cooling. |
+
+Read together, the evidence supports a cool-roof lead because its output and indoor-heat measure can be evaluated within the stated horizon. It supports trees and vegetation as a complement, not as a directly comparable short-term substitute. Neither row is proof of effectiveness: one combines observed need and visual roof observations with a planning model, while the other combines resident preference with an unquantified longer-term benefit.
+
+## East Ward scope
+
+East Ward only is the appropriate starting geography under the corrected decision. In the heat-scan sample, 65% of monitored apartments exceeded 82°F at 11:00 p.m. on at least half of 14 heat-alert days, and the area's median maximum overnight temperature was 84.8°F. Among its survey respondents, 68% reported bedrooms that were too hot after 10:00 p.m. on multiple nights, while 57% said electricity-bill concerns sometimes reduced air-conditioning use. [Housing Heat Scan] [Resident Survey]
+
+Those figures justify focused committee attention, but not neighborhood-wide prevalence estimates. The 40 monitored apartments came from 10 owner-volunteered buildings, and the survey recruitment overlapped with participating buildings. Program reporting should therefore identify the participating cohort rather than imply that its results represent every East Ward renter.
+
+## Three-year program design
+
+1. **Year 1** — Establish the baseline and test feasibility. Assess roof condition at all 10 East Ward buildings represented in the scan, retain hourly apartment monitoring, document weather and building characteristics, and identify tree sites. Use the technical assessment to plan materials, preparation, and installation.
+2. **Year 2** — Complete the modeled first wave of four roofs and plant 60 trees. Track treated roof area separately from indoor-temperature outcomes, continue monitoring through comparable hot-weather periods, and record tree location, species, maintenance, and survival. Where feasible, preserve a later-treated or untreated comparison.
+3. **Year 3** — Complete the remaining three of the modeled seven roofs, reaching the proposed 210,000-square-foot total, and plant the remaining 60 of 120 trees. Evaluate temperature patterns, report tree survival, and return to the committee with observed results, departures from the model, and a continuation recommendation. [Options Memo]
+
+## Measures
+
+The Year 3 values below are proposed targets from the planning memo, not guarantees or observed outcomes. Output measures and temperature outcomes should be reported separately. [Options Memo]
+
+| Measure | Baseline | Year 3 target |
+|---|---|---|
+| Roof area treated | 0 square feet | 210,000 square feet |
+| Maximum overnight indoor temperature | 84.8°F median | 82.8°F or lower |
+| Tree survival | Not applicable | At least 85% of 120 trees |
+
+The temperature comparison should show weather, occupancy, ventilation, air-conditioning use, and building changes where data are available. Because those factors could change, neither the scan nor a later before-and-after difference proves that the program caused or will cause the projected temperature change.
+
+## Limitations
+
+- Sampling: Both the building scan and resident questionnaire used voluntary convenience samples, so neither supports a representative city or neighborhood estimate. [Housing Heat Scan] [Resident Survey]
+- Causality: The scan observed exposure but did not establish that roofs caused it; later temperature changes could also reflect weather, occupancy, ventilation, cooling use, or other building work. [Housing Heat Scan] [Options Memo]
+- Projections: Roof completions, treated area, indoor temperature, tree counts, timing, and survival targets are planning assumptions or proposed targets, not achieved results. [Options Memo]
+
